Jahshaka patch 0083 — the diffuse cone basis is a function of the normal, in the
world.

MEDIA-only patch (one Hlms piece: Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Vct_piece_ps.any).
Studio stages its media from the SOURCE tree (cmake/IncludeOgre.cmake), so every
tree still has to re-run `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` to get it — no engine
COMPILE is needed beyond the staging, which is the whole difference from a
SOURCE patch. It OVERLAPS patches 0021, 0033, 0045, 0048, 0066 and 0070 in that
one file: a tree that already carries those hits `build-ogre.sh`'s "Upstream
changed the patched file" on a re-run, which is the documented per-patch
reverse-check blind spot and NOT an upstream change. The cure is the recorded
one — reset the submodule (`git -C irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next checkout -- . &&
git -C irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next clean -fd`) and re-run the script.

WHAT THE USER SEES (owner review 2026-09-18, screenshots 10 and 11): a hard
DIAGONAL across one flat face of a cube, corner to corner, with a stippled GRAIN
over it — a flat, uniformly lit, single-material quad shaded as two visibly
different triangles.

WHY. `applyVoxelConeTracing` traces six diffuse cones in TANGENT SPACE and needs
a frame to put them in. Upstream handed it whichever frame happened to be in
scope:

  * where the datablock has a normal map (`normal_map`, which every tangented
    mesh in this tree generates) it is the MATERIAL's TBN — `normalize(
    inPs.tangent )` and the binormal built from it. That is a per-VERTEX
    attribute, authored for TEXTURING, and it has three properties that are all
    fine for its own job and all wrong for this one: it is INTERPOLATED, so it
    is a different function inside each of a quad's two triangles (they are
    built from different vertex triples) — the diagonal; it is interpolated PER
    PIXEL and renormalised, so it rotates continuously across a surface — the
    grain; and it follows the UV authoring, so the same box turned on its own
    axis gets a different frame — the same solid shades differently.
  * everywhere else it is `generateTbn`, whose tangent is `OGRE_ddx( pos )`, and
    whose own comment in this file says the result "isn't fully stable due to
    how DDX works and due to floating point precision".

Neither is a defect in ITS OWN job. The defect is that a cone DISTRIBUTION is
not a tangent-space quantity at all: nothing about where the six cones should
point depends on how the artist laid out the UVs. And the error is not small,
because the cone set is not azimuthally symmetric against an ANISOTROPIC voxel
field — the six directional volumes are sampled by three-axis blends, so
rotating the frame about the normal changes which volumes each cone reads.

MEASURED (lane CUBE-SHADE-1, 2026-09-21, a 4 m cube on the default ground at
Epic, 1920x1080, the editor's own grade):

  turning the cube 90 degrees about its own axis — the same solid, in the same
  place, with the same world geometry and the same face normal:

      before   92.9 % of the face's pixels move, worst 16/255, mean 4.8
      after     2.96 %                          , worst  1/255  (the dither)

  the high-frequency energy inside one triangle of the face (mean |p - the mean
  of its two horizontal neighbours|, in display codes):

      before   3.28        GI off (the floor)  0.51
      after    0.49        GI+AO off           0.54

  i.e. the grain is gone to the dither floor, and the diagonal with it.

AND THE FRAME IS ANCHORED IN THE WORLD, WHICH IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE FIX.
Everything in this shader is in VIEW space — the vertex shader multiplies the
normal by worldView, and VctLighting's xform is voxel * view^-1 — so a frame
built from `pixelData.geomNormal` as it stands is a frame that TURNS WITH THE
CAMERA. Frisvad's construction is not rotation-equivariant, so the six cones
would swing about the normal as the head moves: a slow swim of the bounce while
orbiting, and a moving picture on every head turn in VR. That would be a worse
defect than the one this patch is here to fix, and it is not one the material
tangent frame had — a tangent is a world-attached vertex attribute that merely
arrives rotated into view space.

`passBuf.invViewMatCubemap` is the inverse view rotation (with Ogre's
left-handed cubemap z flip) and lives in every non-caster Pbs pass buffer, which
is exactly the set of passes this piece runs in. Read vector-first it takes a
view-space direction into that camera-independent space — the same line the
env-probe piece uses a few hundred lines below — and read matrix-first it brings
one back, the matrix being orthogonal. The frame is built out there and composed
back in ONCE, so the cone loop and every other reader of the matrix are
untouched, at a cost of two 3x3 products per pixel.

  measured, the image-centre probe on a VERTICAL face 0.5 m above the floor,
  with the camera orbiting the point it looks at so that the world point, its
  normal, its neighbours and its voxels are identical at every yaw, each read
  DRAINED until two consecutive reads agree to a quarter of a code:

      camera yaw    0 deg     +30 deg   -30 deg   back to 0 (the floor)
      stock          59.16     55.09     53.37     57.16   -> worst 5.79 (2.00)
      view-anchored  59.16     54.09     53.37     57.16   -> worst 5.79 (2.00)
      world-anchored 81.88     80.88     80.88     80.88   -> worst 1.00 (1.00)

  Two honest notes on that table. The view-anchored arm is NOT separable from
  stock at 30 degrees: the residual both of them carry is the cascade's own
  re-centring as the camera position moves, and it is larger than the frame's
  contribution at this angle, so the magnitude of the temporal artefact is NOT
  established by this measurement — the argument for anchoring is structural,
  and the stability of the third row is what it buys. And a ZERO-YAW camera
  cannot see the difference at all: with no yaw the view x-axis IS world x, so
  for every axis-aligned surface the view-space normal has x = 0 and Frisvad's
  tangent comes out along world x either way. That is exactly why the selftest's
  pose 1 — the camera that has never moved — is byte-identical under this patch
  while the yawed pose 2 is not.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES. `buildConeBasis( n )` is Frisvad's orthonormal basis
(JGT 2012), continuous over the whole sphere but for ONE direction, where a
branch returns a fixed frame instead of dividing by zero.

THE SPECIAL DIRECTION IS A WORLD AXIS, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT A
DEFAULT. The hairy-ball theorem says some direction has to be special; now that
the frame is world-anchored, that is a fixed direction OF THE WORLD, so it is a
real choice. The obvious one is to steer it onto a body diagonal, which no
axis-aligned surface has: a fixed pre-rotation of the space does it in four
lines. That was built and measured, and it COSTS LIGHT. The six cones of an
axis-aligned surface then all point at odd angles, where every sample is a
three-way blend of the anisotropic voxel volumes instead of a near-direct read
of one, and the bounce comes out systematically dimmer:

    gi.field_follows' own bar — the red a wall bounces onto grey ground beyond
    cascade 0, against a GI-off control of 0.0000:

        stock cone basis                  0.0174   (suite passes)
        world-anchored, on the axes       0.0174   (suite passes)
        world-anchored, body diagonal     0.0105   (suite REDS, bar 0.012)

    i.e. forty per cent of the bounce, on every surface in every scene. The
    default scene's selftest said the same thing more quietly: with the body
    diagonal, 44,781 of the 44,786 pixels that moved got DARKER.

So the axes stay. The price is stated rather than hidden: the special direction
is a world axis, so a surface whose normal is within a hair of it takes the
branch's fixed frame while its neighbours take a rapidly turning one. The two
properties cannot be separated — "the frame is axis-aligned for axis normals"
and "the reference direction is an axis" are the same statement — so this is a
trade, made on the side the measurement points to. Duff's branchless variant is
refused for a reason that survives either choice: its sign flip makes the
discontinuity a whole GREAT CIRCLE instead of a point.

THE OTHER TRADE, STATED. A world-anchored frame is invariant under camera
motion and NOT under rotating the whole scene about an axis; a view-anchored one
is the reverse. Cameras move every frame and whole scenes are rotated never, so
this is the side to be on — but it is a choice, not a free lunch, and the
hairy-ball residue is where it is paid.

The material's own TBN is untouched and goes on doing its one job (turning a
tangent-space normal map into a world-space normal). `generateTbn` had exactly
one call site, which is this one, and goes with it.

--- a/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Vct_piece_ps.any
+++ b/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Vct_piece_ps.any
@@ -59,19 +59,68 @@
 		float travelled;
 	};
 
-	/// Generates a TBN matrix when no tangent information is present using
-	/// DDX information.
-	/// Should only we used as a fallback as the result isn't fully stable due to how DDX works
-	/// and due to floating point precision. It is possible for the TBN to change as you zoom-in
-	/// to the triangle.
-	INLINE float3x3 generateTbn( float3 geomNormal, float3 pos )
+	/// Jahshaka patch 0083: THE CONE BASIS IS A FUNCTION OF THE NORMAL AND OF
+	/// NOTHING ELSE. `buildConeBasis` returns an orthonormal frame whose third
+	/// axis is `n`, built by Frisvad's method (JGT 2012) -- continuous over the
+	/// whole sphere but for ONE direction, where the branch below hands back a
+	/// fixed frame instead of dividing by zero.
+	///
+	/// `n` is expected in a CAMERA-INDEPENDENT space (the caller hands it the
+	/// normal taken to cubemap space), because a frame is only worth building
+	/// once it cannot turn with the head.
+	///
+	/// AND IT IS BUILT ABOUT THAT SPACE'S OWN AXES, which is a decision that was
+	/// measured rather than argued. Frisvad's construction is written about
+	/// (0,0,1) and its one special direction is the antipode, so a fixed
+	/// pre-rotation would move that direction anywhere -- onto a body diagonal,
+	/// say, which no axis-aligned surface has. That was tried, and it COSTS
+	/// LIGHT: the six cones of an axis-aligned surface then all point at odd
+	/// angles, where every sample is a three-way blend of the anisotropic voxel
+	/// volumes instead of a near-direct read of one, and the bounce comes out
+	/// systematically dimmer. Measured on gi.field_follows' own bar -- the red a
+	/// wall bounces onto grey ground beyond cascade 0 -- 0.0174 with the axes,
+	/// 0.0105 with the body diagonal: forty per cent of the bounce, gone, on
+	/// every surface in every scene.
+	///
+	/// WHAT THAT BUYS AND WHAT IT COSTS. Keeping the axes keeps the common case
+	/// (walls, floors, ceilings) on the voxel volumes' own axes. The price is
+	/// that the special direction is a world axis: a surface whose normal is
+	/// within a hair of it takes the branch's fixed frame while its neighbours
+	/// take a rapidly turning one. The two properties cannot be separated --
+	/// "the frame is axis-aligned for axis normals" and "the reference direction
+	/// is an axis" are the same statement -- so this is a trade, and it is made
+	/// on the side the measurement points to.
+	///
+	/// The two frames this REPLACES were both unstable, and the instability is
+	/// visible because the diffuse cone set is not azimuthally symmetric against
+	/// an anisotropic voxel field: rotate the frame about the normal and the six
+	/// cones read different axis volumes.
+	///   * the material's TANGENT frame (what the normal_map permutations used):
+	///     a per-VERTEX attribute authored for texturing, interpolated from a
+	///     different vertex triple in each triangle of a quad, so one flat face
+	///     shaded as two triangles with a hard kink along its own diagonal, with
+	///     per-pixel grain across each, and the same box shaded differently after
+	///     being turned on its own axis.
+	///   * the DDX fallback (what the rest used), whose own comment above said it
+	///     "isn't fully stable due to how DDX works and due to floating point
+	///     precision".
+	INLINE float3x3 buildConeBasis( float3 n )
 	{
-		float3 tangentX = OGRE_ddx( pos.xyz ).xyz;
-		tangentX = normalize( tangentX );
-
-		float3 binormal = cross( geomNormal, tangentX ) ;
-		float3x3 TBN = buildFloat3x3( tangentX, binormal, geomNormal );
-		return TBN;
+		float3 t;
+		float3 b;
+		if( n.z < -0.9999999 )
+		{
+			t = float3( 0.0, -1.0, 0.0 );
+			b = float3( -1.0, 0.0, 0.0 );
+		}
+		else
+		{
+			float a = 1.0 / ( 1.0 + n.z );
+			float c = -n.x * n.y * a;
+			t = float3( 1.0 - n.x * n.x * a, c, -n.x );
+			b = float3( c, 1.0 - n.y * n.y * a, -n.y );
+		}
+		return buildFloat3x3( t, b, n );
 	}
 
 	@foreach( 2, n )
@@ -790,9 +839,32 @@
 @end
 
 @piece( applyVoxelConeTracing )
-	@property( !normal_map )
-		float3x3 TBN = generateTbn( pixelData.geomNormal, inPs.pos );
-	@end
+	// Jahshaka patch 0083: the cones get their own frame, and THE FRAME IS
+	// ANCHORED IN THE WORLD, not in the camera.
+	//
+	// Everything in this shader is in VIEW space -- the vertex shader
+	// multiplies the normal by worldView, and the voxel transform
+	// (VctLighting's xform = voxel * view^-1) takes view-space directions to
+	// voxel space -- so a frame built from `pixelData.geomNormal` as it stands
+	// would turn about the normal with the camera's own rotation. For a level
+	// camera the floor's tangent would simply BE the camera's right vector, and
+	// the bounce would swim as you orbit and move on every head turn in VR.
+	// That is a worse defect than the one this patch is here to fix, and it is
+	// not one the material tangent frame had: a tangent is a world-attached
+	// vertex attribute that merely arrives rotated into view space.
+	//
+	// `passBuf.invViewMatCubemap` is the inverse view rotation (with Ogre's
+	// left-handed cubemap z flip) and is in every non-caster Pbs pass buffer,
+	// which is exactly the set of passes this piece runs in. Read
+	// vector-first it takes a view-space direction to that camera-independent
+	// space -- the same line the env-probe piece uses a few hundred lines below
+	// -- and read matrix-first it brings one back, the matrix being orthogonal.
+	// So the frame is built out there and composed back in ONCE, which leaves
+	// the cone loop and every other reader of this matrix untouched.
+	float3x3 jahConeToView = passBuf.invViewMatCubemap;
+	float3x3 jahConeBasis =
+		mul( jahConeToView,
+			 buildConeBasis( mul( float3( pixelData.geomNormal ), jahConeToView ) ) );
 
 	@property( use_parallax_correct_cubemaps )
 		float3 vctSpecPosVS = float3( 0, 0, 0 );
